**Perfect blackbody.** A body which absorbs the ENTIRE radiant energy incident on it is called an ideal or perfect blackbody. For it the coefficient of absorption \( a = 1 \), and therefore \( r = 0 \) and \( t_{\mathrm{r}} = 0 \). Since it reflects nothing, such a surface appears black — unless its temperature is so high that it is self-luminous. By Kirchhoff's law a perfect blackbody is also the best possible EMITTER: at a given temperature it has the maximum emissive power of any body, and its emissivity \( e = 1 \).

**How it is realized in practice.** No material is a perfect blackbody — lamp black and platinum black, which absorb nearly 97% of the incident radiant heat, only come close. A near-perfect blackbody is therefore realized not as a surface but as a CAVITY WITH A SMALL APERTURE, the aperture behaving as the blackbody. The standard arrangement is **Ferry's blackbody**:

- a double-walled hollow sphere with a tiny hole (aperture) through which radiant heat can enter;
- the space between the walls is evacuated and the outer surface is silvered, to cut heat loss;
- the inner surface is coated with lamp black, so that each reflection absorbs most of the remaining energy;
- there is a conical projection on the inner surface directly opposite the aperture. This ensures that a ray entering along the axis of the aperture does not strike the surface normally and so is not reflected straight back out along its own path.

A heat ray entering the aperture therefore suffers multiple reflections inside and is almost completely absorbed; it has a negligible chance of escaping through the small hole. Thus the aperture behaves as a perfect blackbody. Wien used a similar construction with a cylindrical body and a vertical slit as the aperture, which gives a greater effective area. The same effect is obtained with a cavity radiator — a block of material with an internal cavity connected to the outside by a small hole.
